            "text_89QDy6": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>A hot flash is a sudden feeling of heat, usually in the face, neck and chest, often with flushing and sweating. Most last between one and five minutes. During perimenopause, changing estrogen levels affect the part of the brain that regulates body temperature, so its comfort zone narrows and small changes set off a heat response. Hot flashes that happen during sleep are called night sweats.</p><p>They are common. In a 2025 survey of more than 17,000 women, about seven in ten women over 35 reported hot flashes or night sweats. For most women they ease with time, but \"common\" does not mean you have to just live with them.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
